---
description: Guidelines for creating and maintaining Cursor rules to ensure consistency and effectiveness.
globs: .cursor/rules/*.mdc
alwaysApply: true
---

- **Required Rule Structure:**
  ```markdown
  ---
  description: Clear, one-line description of what the rule enforces
  globs: path/to/files/*.ext, other/path/**/*
  alwaysApply: boolean
  ---

  - **Main Points in Bold**
    - Sub-points with details
    - Examples and explanations
  ```

- **File References:**
  - Use `[filename](mdc:Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/path/to/file)` ([filename](mdc:Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/filename)) to reference files
  - Example: [prisma.mdc](mdc:Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/.cursor/rules/prisma.mdc) for rule references
  - Example: [schema.prisma](mdc:Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/Sirius/prisma/schema.prisma) for code references

- **Code Examples:**
  - Use language-specific code blocks
  ```typescript
  // ✅ DO: Show good examples
  const goodExample = true;
  
  // ❌ DON'T: Show anti-patterns
  const badExample = false;
  ```

- **Development Guide**
  - Apps are either frontend or backend. Backend app development happens inside of the sirius-engine container. This container has the associated directory mounted at `/app-agent` for dev mode. This means that local changes to the code will be reflected in the docker container at `/app-agent`. Any troubleshooting or restart commands MUST be executed in the container (for example: `docker exec sirius-engine cat /app-agent/go.mod`)
  
  - **Project Structure**
    - Follow standard Go project layout:
      ```
      .
      ├── cmd/                 # Main applications
      │   └── app-name/       # Application entry points
      ├── internal/           # Private application code
      │   ├── agent/         # Core business logic
      │   └── config/        # Configuration management
      └── pkg/               # Public library code
          ├── queue/         # Queue integration
          └── store/         # Storage integration
      ```
    - Use meaningful package names that reflect their purpose
    - Keep packages focused and cohesive

  - **Testing Best Practices**
    - Write descriptive, visual test output:
      ```go
      t.Log("\n🔍 Testing component...")
      t.Logf("\nConfiguration:\n  %s", config)
      t.Log("\n✅ Test passed" or "\n❌ Test failed")
      ```
    - Use table-driven tests for comprehensive coverage
    - Test both success and error cases
    - Mock external dependencies appropriately
    - Use clear visual separators between test sections
    - Include test configuration details in output
    - Show clear success/failure indicators with emojis
    - Document expected vs actual results
    - Use horizontal lines to separate test cases

  - **Error Handling**
    - Return errors with context using fmt.Errorf
    - Log errors at appropriate levels
    - Use structured logging with slog
    - Validate configuration early
    - Implement graceful shutdown
    - Handle context cancellation

  - **Configuration Management**
    - Use environment variables for configuration
    - Validate all required configuration values
    - Provide clear error messages for missing config
    - Use strong typing for configuration values
    - Document all configuration options

  - **Code Organization**
    - Follow Go idioms and conventions
    - Use dependency injection for better testing
    - Implement graceful startup/shutdown
    - Use context for cancellation
    - Keep functions focused and small
    - Document exported types and functions

- **Rule Content Guidelines:**
  - Start with high-level overview
  - Include specific, actionable requirements
  - Show examples of correct implementation
  - Reference existing code when possible
  - Keep rules DRY by referencing other rules

- **Rule Maintenance:**
  - Update rules when new patterns emerge
  - Add examples from actual codebase
  - Remove outdated patterns
  - Cross-reference related rules

- **Best Practices:**
  - Use bullet points for clarity
  - Keep descriptions concise
  - Include both DO and DON'T examples
  - Reference actual code over theoretical examples
  - Use consistent formatting across rules 
